A||||11||tomcat06085||88 GT under 2500 lbs for autocrossng?||||5||
Z||000000||tomcat06085||12-18-2001||05:56 PM||||I am preparing an 88 GT for autocrossing in the spring. Is there any legal way to get the weight under 2500 pounds and still be legal by Solo2 rules. I think the weight is around 2750 for the road. I have done this before and will anticipate the obvious things like removing tols, spare tire, etc., but want to compete in the stock class.<P>------------------<BR><BR> <IMG SRC="http://jrreeves.com/images/blckfire.jpg"> <BR>tomcat...Thanks for the help!||||reg||5
Z||000001||shark93726||12-18-2001||08:57 PM||||A few things to think about.<P>Take out the air conditioning system and radio, they are optional and are not required.<P>Use 14" X 7" wheels instead of the 15" ones and Hoosier low profile D.O.T. racing tires, that will reduce wheel and tire weight, and will lower the car for better handling.<P>Turn the brake rotors to absolute minimum thickness to reduce weight even more.<P>The steering wheel can be exchanged as long as the replacement is within 1" in diameter of stock, get the absolute lightest one available.<P>I believe seats can be exchanged now too, but they have to be fully upholstered ones, so if you can find competition type upholstered lightweight ones, that could be a lot of help.<P>You better check the new rule book though, they change things every year, and you don't want to get protested out of class for a minor infraction. <P>------------------<BR>Gerald Storvik (8shark.com)||||reg||1
Z||000002||honegod||12-18-2001||10:37 PM||||if a seat swap is permissable, the seats from a 914 porsche would be an EASY drop in, and they weigh next to nothing.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Blacktree||12-19-2001||12:18 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Take out the air conditioning system and radio, they are optional and are not required.<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>Definately get rid of the A/C system... all of it (compressor, mounting brackets, hoses & tubes, evaporator, condenser, swap the heater core for a non-AC heater core, etc). Basically, just convert the car to the non-AC model. You should drop about 100 lb right there. <P>If you pull out the entire sound system (including the speaker wires and stuff), that should save you about another 40-50 lb or so.<P>If the car has power windows and locks, you should be able to remove those as well (weren't they optional?). You might save another 25-30 lb there.<P>The smaller rims with low-profile tires is a good suggestion, also. I believe the aluminum sport rims are a tad bit lighter than the stamped steel rims.<P>------------------<BR><A HREF="http://www.tweaktech.com" TARGET=_blank>Tweaktech</A>||||reg||1
Z||000004||Will||12-19-2001||12:54 AM||||What CLASS in solo2 are you running?<BR>None of what you mentioned is legal in C-stock, but I guess you know that.<BR>I'm not sure about the legalities of turning a car equipped with A/C by the factory into a non-AC car using stock parts...<P>Stripping interior, lightening holes, etc aren't allowed in C-street prepared, either.<P>Radical weight reduction is only allowed in prepared or modified classes, and you'd better do a LOT more than just weight reduction if you want to be competitive in those classes.||||reg||1
Z||000005||fieroX||12-19-2001||03:35 AM||||Wouldnt the car handle better with real light 17's rather than the stock 14's?  The rims I have are the same weight as the stockers.  Or do they have to be stock?<P>------------------<BR>    <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroX.com" TARGET=_blank>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fierox.com/logo2small.gif">  </A><BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroX.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroX.com</A><BR>coming soon L67 powered 87 coupe||||reg||1
Z||000006||SLYGUY||12-19-2001||03:53 AM||||What is Autocross?<P>We get a lot or rice rocket racing around here but never heard of that type of racing before I read of it here.<P>Sly||||reg||1
Z||000007||tomcat06085||12-19-2001||06:47 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Will:<BR><B>What CLASS in solo2 are you running?<BR>None of what you mentioned is legal in C-stock, but I guess you know that.<BR>I'm not sure about the legalities of turning a car equipped with A/C by the factory into a non-AC car using stock parts...<P>Stripping interior, lightening holes, etc aren't allowed in C-street prepared, either.<P>Radical weight reduction is only allowed in prepared or modified classes, and you'd better do a LOT more than just weight reduction if you want to be competitive in those classes.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>The car will compete in C-Stock and it has most of the options. I know one of the clubs I've been watching won't allow seat changes, but the a/c and stero equipment can be removed. Years ago, when I autocrossed, the guys would keep pushing the limits of the rules until someone complained. We don't want to push the limits, but want to carry as little weight as possible, along with engine, suspension tuning,along with the stickiest, lightest tires best suited for the car. Heard that Hoosier is best all around autocross tire these days.<BR>||||reg||3
Z||000008||KRMFiero||12-19-2001||07:51 PM||||I know nothing about autocross? anyone have any sites?<P>anyways i doubt this is leagle but pull the door panels off and cut out that huge hevey beam out, replace with some nice lighter steel or bolt in aluminum or something,    taking those beams out should save atlead 50 lbs||||reg||1
Z||000009||Dough19||12-19-2001||08:18 PM||||[QUOTE]Originally posted by KRMFiero:<BR>[B]I know nothing about autocross? anyone have any sites?<P>try <A HREF="http://www.autocross.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.autocross.com</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000010||tomcat06085||12-25-2001||06:36 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by KRMFiero:<BR><B>I know nothing about autocross? anyone have any sites?<P>anyways i doubt this is leagle but pull the door panels off and cut out that huge hevey beam out, replace with some nice lighter steel or bolt in aluminum or something,    taking those beams out should save atlead 50 lbs</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Taking door panels off would put you into a street prepared or modified class. If I did this, it would definitely make me go all out and gut the car. I really would like to compete in the C-stock class.<P>Try this link for some info on autocross and solo racing.   <A HREF="http://www.cartct.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.cartct.com/</A> <P>------------------<P>  <IMG SRC="http://jrreeves.com/images/blckfire.jpg">  <BR>tomcat...Thanks for the help!<p>[This message has been edited by tomcat06085 (edited 12-25-2001).]||||reg||3
Z||000011||cadero2dmax||12-26-2001||06:55 PM||||Reading some of the replies you are receiving, I would suggest that you take your question to: FieroRacingList@yahoogroups.com<P>These guys can give you straight answers, based on experience, because they have been there - done that!!<P>G<P>------------------<BR>George Ryan<BR>A pair of 4.9 Cadero's<P>For Racing Photos of my former Q4 Fiero racecar, go to: <BR>http://albums.photopoint.com/j/AlbumIndex?u=142520&a=1342821 <P>(until I can figure out how to post photos)||||reg||1
